        <b>Rounding</b>
        <span>In “Ledger owns rounding”<span data-k="doc-working" data-gone> · agent working</span></span>
      </div>
      <article class="doc">
        <h4>Rounding</h4>
        <p>
          Money is stored in minor units: cents, pence, yen. Every amount that reaches the
          <a>Ledger</a> is already an integer, so rounding happens before a value is written, never
          after.
        </p>
        <h5>Where it happens</h5>
        <p class="doc__edit">
          <span data-k="old" data-gone>Each invoice line rounds its own total. </span><span class="doc__gone" data-k="ghost" data-gone>Each invoice line rounds its own total.</span><span class="doc__typed" data-k="ins-1">Rounding happens once, in the ledger,</span><span class="doc__typed" data-k="ins-2"> using half to even.</span><span class="doc__typed" data-k="ins-3"> Invoices pass exact totals and let the ledger round them,</span><span class="doc__typed" data-k="ins-4"> so a long invoice no longer drifts a cent per line.</span><span class="cursor" data-k="cursor" data-gone><span class="cursor__flag">claude-opus-4-6</span></span>
        </p>
        <h5>Why half to even</h5>
        <p>
          Rounding half up nudges totals upward across many entries. Half to even matches how
          existing entries were written, which <a>Reconciliation</a> depends on.
        </p>
      </article>
